  "text_md": "Remark 2.14. In PLfo,1js; the evaluation of formulas with connective is of formulas with connective A keeping the unification of form of the normal encoding function; we extend the evaluation of formulas with connective ^ as lla ^bl = which will not cause any confusion in this paper\n\n## 2.3. Model Checking\n\nBesnard and Doutre [12] give a method of model checking. The method aims to translate an AF as propositional formula in the PL2; such that the models of the propositional formula are corresponding to extensions of the AF under a kind of semantics. In fact, the core of each model is an extension of the AF and vice versa. The translating functions depend on various semantics. The mainly results in the chapter model checking\" in [12] are listed as the following; where $ is a translating function. each\n\n- 1 Translating the AF to the for conflict-free semantics.\n\nLet AF = (A,R) be an argumentation framework.\n\nA set S € A is conflict-free iff S is the core of a model of any formula below:\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\n- 2 Translating the AF to the PLz for stable semantics.\n\nA set $ = A is a stable extension iff S is the core of a model of the formula below:\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\nThis formula is come up with by Creignou in [29] to characterize the kernels of a graph; and is applied in [12] to characterize the stable extensions of an AF .\n\nA set S € A is a admissible set iff S is the core of a model of the formula below:\n\n- 3 Translating the AF to the PL2 for admissible semantics.\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\n- 4 Translating the AF to the PLz for complete semantics .\n\nA set S € A is a complete extension iff S is the core of a model of the formula below:\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->",
